"""Text content of the chapter 1 deck v3.4.0 (a NEW file; task TK_Ch01_DeckTeach_Build): the nine stories told as sentences, and the speaker notes.
Keys of STORY and NOTES are the slide numbers of the deck v3.3.0 (old numbering); NEW_NOTES holds the notes of the 14 slides added in v3.4.0, by key.
Every note is reader's prose of three or four sentences: what to say, what to point at, a question to ask, the common mistake."""
__version__ = "3.4.0"

STORY = {
    7: ("3 January 2009 · block 0 of the Bitcoin chain · Satoshi Nakamoto",
        ["On 3 January 2009 Satoshi Nakamoto mined the first Bitcoin block, called block 0.",
         "He typed a headline into the block's script: The Times, 3 January 2009, Chancellor on brink of second bailout for banks.",
         "The headline proves the block was not made earlier, and it records the banking crisis that Bitcoin answers; the next code slide reads those 69 bytes."],
        "Block 0 is a dated receipt and a message at once, and anyone can read it on a block explorer today."),
    9: ("12 January 2009 · block 170 · Satoshi Nakamoto and Hal Finney",
        ["Nine days after block 0, Satoshi Nakamoto sent 10 bitcoin to the cryptographer Hal Finney in block 170, the first payment between two people.",
         "Finney had answered the white paper with curiosity and had built the first reusable proof-of-work system years before.",
         "He ran the software and wrote ‘Running bitcoin’; the coins reached him directly, with no bank in between."],
        "The first payment already shows the whole idea: two people, one shared ledger and no bank between them."),
    10: ("1982 to 2008 · papers and mailing lists · Chaum, Back, Dai and Szabo",
         ["Between 1982 and 1998 David Chaum, Adam Back, Wei Dai and Nick Szabo each built a part of digital money.",
          "Their systems failed or stayed unbuilt, because none could agree on one history without a trusted leader.",
          "Satoshi Nakamoto's white paper of 31 October 2008 cites them and adds proof of work as the voting weight of an open network."],
         "Bitcoin was less a new invention than a new combination of four near misses."),
    14: ("1903 · Yap, Micronesia · William Henry Furness III",
         ["On the island of Yap, wealth was held in limestone discs called rai, some four metres wide, that never moved when they changed owner.",
          "The anthropologist William Henry Furness III described this after visiting in 1903; a stone that sank at sea was still traded.",
          "What changed was the shared memory of who owned each stone, so the stone worked as a public ledger."],
         "Money is whatever a community agrees to keep a record of: Yap's stones were a ledger long before Bitcoin."),
    15: ("3000 BCE to 15 August 1971 · Mesopotamia to the United States · Richard Nixon",
         ["Grain ledgers, Lydian coins, paper notes and bank balances are all ledgers; they differ in who keeps them.",
          "On 15 August 1971 President Richard Nixon cut the dollar's last tie to gold, leaving pure fiat money.",
          "Cards and online banking made money digital as entries in private ledgers; Bitcoin keeps the ledger in public."],
         "Money has always been a ledger; Bitcoin keeps it in public."),
    17: ("8 July 2002 · Palo Alto and San Jose · Levchin, Thiel and Musk",
         ["On 8 July 2002 eBay agreed to buy PayPal for 1.5 billion dollars, ending the dot-com race to pay online.",
          "PayPal grew from Confinity (Max Levchin and Peter Thiel, 1998) and X.com (Elon Musk, 1999), which merged in 2000; Beenz and Flooz died in the 2001 crash.",
          "Online payment was solved only as accounts on one company's private ledger; the internet still had no cash."],
         "Paying went digital, but every payment still passed through a gatekeeper who could say no."),
    18: ("26 February 2022 · Kyiv, Ukraine · Mykhailo Fedorov",
         ["On 26 February 2022 the government of Ukraine, through Mykhailo Fedorov's ministry of digital transformation, published crypto donation addresses.",
          "With an aid group it raised 54.7 million dollars in about a week, and CNBC reported the total on 3 March 2022.",
          "With war at the capital, ordinary rails were too slow; open digital money kept working where banks, cards or cash stopped."],
         "An open network asks no permission: an address was enough to send help from anywhere."),
    24: ("22 May 2010 · Jacksonville, Florida · Laszlo Hanyecz",
         ["On 22 May 2010 Laszlo Hanyecz paid 10,000 bitcoin for two Papa John's pizzas, the first known commercial purchase in bitcoin.",
          "He had offered the coins on the bitcointalk forum, and the student Jeremy Sturdivant ordered the pizzas for delivery to Jacksonville.",
          "Until then nobody knew what a bitcoin was worth; this purchase is the day a floating price began to exist."],
         "A price is only what two people agree on once; the next slide reprices those coins at six later moments."),
    44: ("28 February 2014 · Tokyo, Japan · Mark Karpeles",
         ["On 28 February 2014 Mt. Gox of Tokyo, led by Mark Karpeles, filed for bankruptcy at the Tokyo District Court and admitted that about 850,000 bitcoin were gone.",
          "Customers had balances on a website, but the keys, and so the coins, were in someone else's hands all along.",
          "Creditors were still being repaid a decade later."],
         "Not your keys, not your coins: a balance on someone else's website is a promise, not a coin."),
}

NOTES = {
1: "This chapter answers one question: what is Bitcoin, and why does every part of it exist? Point at the coin on circuit traces and ask what students already believe Bitcoin is. Common first answers are an app or a company; the better answer is a ledger that many computers keep together.",
2: "Six parts follow the order a first payment needs them. Point at the three counters on the right: slides, true stories and runnable examples. Ask which part students feel least sure about. Every example has a twin in the Chapter Examples tab of the course page, so they can rerun it at home.",
3: "Read the six questions aloud and promise to answer them in the summary. Ask who can already answer the first. A common answer lists inventors; the better one is that Bitcoin combined four earlier parts, which part 2 shows.",
4: "This map is the skeleton of the lecture: one root and six branches. Point at the branch we are about to enter each time we move on. Ask which branch looks hardest. Remind students that the course page has the same map to click through.",
5: "The first part has three beats: the first block, the first payment and the forty years before. Read each comparison aloud. Ask what a time capsule has in common with a block. A common slip is to treat the headline as decoration; it is a dated proof.",
6: "Two cards open the chapter: what the introduction promises and how the history fits. Read each card twice, once for the meaning and once for the comparison. Ask a student to retell the family-tree comparison. The usual mistake is to learn history as dates instead of as parts that Bitcoin reuses.",
7: "Tell this one as a story. Point at the hex dump and at the source link; anyone can open this block today. Ask why a newspaper headline proves a date. A common mistake is to read it as a message to the public; it is a timestamp for the future.",
8: "The code cuts the first block's script into eight bytes of pushes and the text. Point at the result [8, 69] and at the headline in the box beside it. Ask where 77 comes from: eight plus sixty-nine. Students often forget that bytes become text only after decode.",
9: "Two people, one shared ledger, no bank. Point at the photograph of Hal Finney and at block 170 in the link. Ask why the first payment went to a cryptographer. A common mistake is to call Finney the first miner; he ran the software and received the coins.",
10: "Four names, four parts, forty years. Point at the photograph of David Chaum and ask which part each person built. A common mistake is to say Bitcoin invented digital cash; the white paper cites these systems and adds proof of work.",
11: "Read the timeline from 1982 to 2009 and stop at each name. Ask what the four near misses had in common. None could agree on one history without a leader. Students often mix up the dates, so point at 1982, 1997, 1998 and 2008.",
12: "Money is a ledger of who owns what, and Bitcoin keeps that ledger in public. Read the three beats: what money was, units and supply, price and exchange. Ask what a scoreboard and a bank balance have in common. Both work only because everyone trusts the record.",
13: "Four cards: the floating exchange rate, how a fair daily price is found, the currency exchange and the Bitcoin ATM. Ask why the tomato stall is a good comparison for price. Students often think someone sets the price; the next slides show that trades do.",
14: "On Yap the stones stayed where they were and only the memory of ownership moved. Point at the photograph and ask what the ledger was. The shared memory of the village. A common mistake is to focus on the size of the stones instead of the record.",
15: "Walk the timeline from grain ledgers to 15 August 1971. Point at the coin photograph and the link to the money museum. Ask who keeps the ledger in each age. The common slip is to say fiat money has no ledger; bank balances are one.",
16: "This is the chapter's one table. Read the first column down, then compare the three money kinds across each row. Ask who keeps the ledger in cash, and the answer is nobody; possession is the record. Students often miss that Bitcoin settles in about an hour, not instantly.",
17: "Tell the PayPal story as a lesson in gatekeepers. Point at the logo and at the names Levchin, Thiel and Musk. Ask what the internet still lacked after 2002, and the answer is cash that needs no company between payer and payee. A common mistake is to think online payment equals digital cash.",
18: "A war stopped ordinary payments, and an open network kept working. Point at the photograph of Mykhailo Fedorov and the 54.7 million dollars. Ask what the donors needed to send help, and the answer is only an address. Check the CNBC source together; do not repeat figures from memory.",
19: "Five documented cases, each with its own link: Kenya, Cyprus, Greece, Venezuela and Ukraine. Point at one photograph per case. Ask which case is about trust in banks and which about access. A common mistake is to treat these as hypotheticals; every one is sourced.",
20: "A bitcoin is counted in whole satoshis, a hundred million to the coin. Point at the result [100000000, 100000, 1] and at the exact conversion of 0.001. Ask why exact whole numbers matter for money. Floating-point decimals would drift; students often type 0.001 as a float.",
21: "The subsidy halves every 210,000 blocks. Point at the five heights and at the result 50, 25, 12.5, 6.25, 3.125. Ask what the next value is, and the answer is half of 3.125. Students often think halving happens by date; it happens by block height.",
22: "The cap is arithmetic, not a promise. Point at the one-line sum over 33 eras and at the result, just under 21 million. Ask why it is not exactly 21 million. Each era drops fractions below one satoshi, so the total ends at 20,999,999.9769.",
23: "A price is a ratio, and a day's figure is a weighted average. Point at the three trades and the result 62,764.71. Ask why a plain average would mislead. The big trade of one bitcoin should count more than the small one of 0.2.",
24: "The first purchase made a price exist. Point at the plaque photograph and the Guinness link. Ask what Hanyecz needed to know before he could set a price, and the answer is nothing yet existed to compare with. A common mistake is to say the pizzas were worth a lot; the point is the first price.",
25: "The same ten thousand coins at six moments, drawn on a log axis. Point at the first bar, 41 dollars, and the last. Ask why a log axis is used. The values span seven orders of magnitude; on a plain axis the early bars would vanish.",
26: "No bank sits in the middle: shared rules and shared work do its job. Read the three beats. Ask what neighbours who all keep a land register have in common with nodes, and the answer is each can check the others. A common mistake is to think a node is a server owned by Bitcoin.",
27: "Compare the bank in the middle with peers. Point at the single ledger-keeper on the left and at the ring of nodes on the right. Ask what happens if one node disappears, and the answer is nothing; the network still agrees. Decentralisation here is an architecture, not a slogan.",
28: "Three cards: proof of work, consensus and the double-spend. Read the jigsaw, the neighbours and the photocopied banknote. Ask why a hard puzzle that is easy to check is useful. Students often think proof of work solves something useful; its value is the cost.",
29: "A toy proof of work with hexadecimal zeros. Point at the result [9, 223, 1208, 57247]: each extra zero costs about sixteen times more guesses. Ask how many guesses checking needs, and the answer is one hash. Students often expect the search to be exactly sixteen times longer; it is an average.",
30: "Mining pays for honesty. Follow the arrows from transactions waiting in the mempool to miners, a block about every ten minutes, and the reward. Ask why cheating costs more than it could win. Honest blocks pay in bitcoin; a rewrite spends energy for nothing.",
31: "A digital coin is a file, and files copy perfectly. Walk the three steps down the left, then the picture on the right where one coin reaches two shops. Ask what stopped this before Bitcoin, and the answer is a trusted central ledger. The mistake is to think encryption alone prevents copying.",
32: "The double-spend is solved in public. Follow the numbered steps: every node hears both payments, they agree on one order, the first enters the block and the second is refused. Ask who plays referee. Nobody; the shared order does it.",
33: "One shared ledger, one rule. Point at the second pay call and at the result {'c1': 'bob'}: Carol never receives the coin. Ask what would happen without the rule, and the answer is both shops would see valid money. Students often expect an error message; the refusal is silent in this toy.",
34: "Each block hashes its text with the block before. Point at the change to block two and at the result 1. Ask which blocks are no longer trusted, and the answer is block two and everything after it. Students often think only the changed block fails.",
35: "Two cards: confirmation and transaction fee. Read the cement comparison and the tip for the courier. Ask why a payment is not final the moment it is seen, and the answer is a later block could still replace it. Common mistake: confusing the fee with the amount paid to the receiver.",
36: "A transaction has no fee field. Point at the two outputs, 20,000 and 75,000, and at the result 5,000. Ask where a miner finds the fee, and the answer is it is whatever the outputs leave over. A forgotten change output turns into an expensive gift.",
37: "A wallet holds keys, not coins, like a ring of keys to many safes. Read the three beats. Ask what a pocket, a safe and a cloakroom ticket say about risk. Students often picture coins sitting inside the app; the coins live on the chain.",
38: "Four questions place any wallet: platform, node type, key control and backup. Point at the four branches. Ask a student to place the phone app they use. Students often mix up node type and key control; they are independent choices.",
39: "Three cards: getting started, choosing a wallet and the types of wallet, plus the desktop wallet. Read the bag, pocket and bicycle comparisons. Ask what makes one kind riskier. Who holds the keys and where the device runs.",
40: "Four more kinds: mobile, web, hardware signing device and third-party API client. Ask which one is the cloakroom. The web wallet. A common mistake is to think a hardware wallet stores coins; it keeps keys and signs.",
41: "Full node, lightweight client, the comparison between them and who controls the keys. Ask who rereads every page of the ledger. The full node. Point at the last card: whoever holds the keys controls the coins.",
42: "With a house the key opens and the registry proves; in Bitcoin the key does both. Point at the two columns and at the word deed. Ask what proof of ownership exists beyond the key, and the answer is none. A signature proves you hold the key while the key stays secret.",
43: "Custody is the first security decision. Compare full control with third-party control and point at the four device icons. Ask who can freeze coins in each column, and the answer is only the service in the right one. Students underestimate that backup becomes their own job.",
44: "Tell the Mt. Gox story with the date. Point at the logo and at the Tokyo District Court link. Ask where the keys were, and the answer is in the exchange's hands, not the customers'. A common mistake is to say the bitcoin were hacked on the blockchain; the loss was custody.",
45: "Two cards: the noncustodial wallet and the recovery code. Read the home safe and the master key in the locker. Ask what twelve words can rebuild, and the answer is every key of the wallet. A common mistake is to photograph the words and store them online.",
46: "A recovery phrase is stretched into a seed. Point at the three seeds for 1, 10 and 2048 rounds. Ask why slow is good here, and the answer is every guess of an attacker costs 2048 rounds. Students often think the seed is the phrase itself; it is derived from it.",
47: "From an empty wallet to a first payment, like a first driving lesson. Read the three beats. Ask what the first thing to do is, and the answer is write down the recovery code. A common slip is to buy bitcoin before the wallet is backed up.",
48: "Three cards: the quick start, Bitcoin addresses and the invoice. Read the new locker number for each delivery and the bill. Ask why a fresh address for each payer helps. It keeps payments from being linked to one another.",
49: "Alice's first bitcoin in four steps: install, address, acquire, confirm. Point at the recovery code note under step one and at the confirmations note. Ask why no account is opened. An address is derived from a key and registered nowhere.",
50: "An address is a hash with a checksum. Point at the four lines: version, payload, checksum, Base58. Ask why a mistyped address is caught, and the answer is the last four bytes check the rest. Students often think the address is the public key; it is a hash of it.",
51: "An invoice is a link with an amount. Point at the parsed fields and at the result 15,000 satoshis. Ask what a QR code adds, and the answer is nothing; it is the same text drawn as dots. Students sometimes forget to convert bitcoin to satoshis exactly.",
52: "Three cards: receiving bitcoin, getting your first bitcoin and sending and receiving. Read the payment slip, the foreign money and the till. Ask which way suits a student with no bitcoin. Buying from a friend is the simplest.",
53: "A wallet spends whole coins and returns the change. Point at the three coins and at the result 18,500. Ask why the wallet picks 80,000 and not 50,000, and the answer is that 50,000 does not cover 61,500. A common mistake is to think a coin can be cut.",
54: "Two old solved problems joined into one system, like two rivers. Read the three beats. Ask which two problems are meant, and the answer is making spam cost work, and agreeing without trust. Students often think Bitcoin solved something nobody had tried.",
55: "Hashcash from 1997 and the Byzantine generals from 1982 meet in 2008. Point at the two boxes. Ask what each contributes, and the answer is a cost for sending, and agreement without a leader. Neither idea was new; their combination was.",
56: "The pipeline the rest of the course walks: keys, addresses, transactions, blocks, mining. Point at the chapter number under each box. Ask which box this chapter has touched already. Each stage trusts only what it can verify.",
57: "Offchain technology in one card, with Alice, Bob, Carol and Dave in a picture. Read the bar tab comparison. Ask what the chain sees when Alice pays Dave through three channels, and the answer is nothing. Students often think offchain means unsafe.",
58: "A channel costs two transactions however many payments follow. Point at the result [-1, 8, 98, 998] and at the negative first value. Ask when a channel starts to pay off, and the answer is from the third payment. The first value is negative: one payment alone loses.",
59: "Bitcoin's stated characteristics and the architecture behind them. Point at virtual, borderless, decentralized and robust, then at the three structures below. Ask which structure delivers which adjective. Each is engineered, not advertised.",
60: "Six lines, one per part. Read each line and point to the slide that proves it. Ask students to say one line in their own words without looking. If a line feels unproven, its code slide carries the receipt.",
61: "Resources for further reading and watching: the white paper, the book, the developer guides, explorers, the community and the seminal papers. Ask each student to look up block 0 on mempool.space. Every entry is a live link in this file.",
62: "Close with the six questions from the start and ask who can now answer each. Give a minute for questions. The aim is that every student can say, in one sentence each, where Bitcoin came from, who holds keys and how a double spend is refused.",
}

NEW_NOTES = {
"M1": "A central bank decides how much money exists and a clearing house settles payments; Bitcoin does both by rule. Point at the three rows. Ask which institution each row replaces. Deflationary is a tendency of a fixed supply, not a promise of rising prices.",
"D1": "A price is where a buyer and a seller meet. Point at the gap between the highest bid and the lowest ask in the toy book. Ask what happens when a buyer raises the bid to the ask, and the answer is a trade, and that trade becomes the last price. The numbers are illustrative.",
"M2": "Difficulty adjustment keeps blocks near ten minutes whatever the total computing power. Point at the two cases: blocks too fast, target gets harder; too slow, easier. Ask why it needs 2016 blocks of evidence. One block is luck; two weeks of blocks are a measurement.",
"B1": "A block holds a time and transactions, and carries the fingerprint of the block before. Point at the arrows that run backwards. Ask what happens when an old block is edited, and the answer is its fingerprint changes and every later link fails. A timestamp orders blocks; it does not make them true.",
"E1": "Each block built on top adds another confirmation. Point at the staircase and at six confirmations, about an hour. Ask whether a payment is ever one hundred percent final, and the answer is it is only more and more costly to reverse. Irreversible here means practically, not magically.",
"W1": "Five kinds of wallet side by side. Read across a row: where the keys live, the main risk, the everyday comparison. Ask which row suits a small daily amount and which a large savings. A common mistake is to think a hardware wallet holds coins.",
"W2": "A full node checks every rule itself; a lightweight client trusts the nodes it asks. Point at the trust row. Ask what you give up for convenience, and the answer is independence and some privacy. Students often think a lightweight wallet has no keys; it keeps them.",
"T1": "Two common attacks: phishing asks you for the secret, malware steals it or changes the address you paste. Point at the three defences. Ask why no honest wallet ever asks for the recovery code in a web form. Anyone who has it owns the coins.",
"A2": "Reusing an address lets anyone link and total your payments. Point at the left column where three payers all use one address and at the right where each payer gets a fresh one. Ask what an onlooker learns in each case. Fresh addresses keep payments apart.",
"A1": "Four ways to a first bitcoin: buy from a friend, earn it, use an ATM or use an exchange. Read the third column: what each needs from you. Ask which one is simplest, and the answer is a friend. Note that a balance at an exchange is not your keys; recall Mt. Gox.",
"K1": "Five cryptographic tools in one picture. Point at the two real hashes: one changed character gives an unrelated fingerprint. Ask which tool proves who signed, and the answer is the digital signature. Students often mix up a hash, which has no key, with a keyed hash, which does.",
"C1": "Everyday computing words under this chapter. Read a row at a time and tie each to where it appeared: explorers in a browser, the node database, a hash of 32 bytes. Ask which term a student is unsure of. Do not skip bits and bytes; later chapters count in them.",
"S1": "Bitcoin changes without a boss through open proposals. Follow the flow from idea to BIP to reference implementation to each node's decision. Ask who forces an upgrade, and the answer is nobody; each operator chooses. The W3C row shows the same open habit for identity.",
"O1": "A payment channel costs two on-chain transactions however many payments follow. Follow open, update, close. Ask what the Lightning Network adds, and the answer is channels linked so that any peer can pay any other. Offchain also changes what a backup must cover.",
}
